+  // vibeos-local (M14 WoW64/FEX startup flake): never hand the dispatcher a null
+  // host block. The ExitFunctionLinker thunk does `br x0` on our return value
+  // unconditionally (Dispatcher.cpp), so a 0 here becomes `br 0` -> host PC=0 ->
+  // the kernel kills the WoW64 process at startup. Under multi-VM host
+  // contention a guest's __alloca/_chkstk `ret` transiently resolves a 0 branch
+  // target for GuestRip whose stack slot actually holds the real (valid) return
+  // address (kernel [UEXC-KILL] dump: Stk+0 = correct return addr while the JIT
+  // read 0), so CompileBlock legitimately fails to produce a block. The guest
+  // RIP is valid, so re-entering the dispatcher loop re-reads State.rip and
+  // recompiles cleanly once the transient clears — the same recovery the TF
+  // path above already uses (`return DispatcherLoopTop`). Upstream FEX assumes
+  // CompileBlock never fails for a mapped RIP (true on Linux, where the frontend
+  // read faults the page in); the Windows/WOW64 compile path can observe the
+  // transient. Local fix, not for upstream (vendor/fex is vendored).
+  if (!HostCode) {
+    return Frame->Pointers.DispatcherLoopTop;
+  }
